  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe upper level low pressure over southern Saskatchewan will move \neast to western Ontario by late tonight, weakening as it continues \nto occlude. Deep moisture is expected to remain in place as mid-\nlevel lapse rates steepen as instability increases to 2500-3000 J/kg \nover the south-central U.P. by the most likely time of initiation \naround 00z. Surface-500mb shear is expected to increase to 40-45kts \nby this time as well. Thus, the instability and shear are present \nfor the potential development of a severe thunderstorm, if initiation \noccurs. However, given that most models indicate a capping inversion \nover much of the central U.P. through this evening and that only \nminimal lift is expected as the boundary continues to occlude as it \npasses through, thunderstorm development could be limited to only \nthose storms that are strong enough to break the cap. The 1630z \nupdate of the SPC outlook continues the Marginal (5%) chance for \nsever thunderstorms across the central U.P. The main hazards with \nany storms that develop will be large hail, damaging wind, and heavy \nrainfall.\n\nLows tonight are expected to be in the upper 50s inland and low 60s \nnear the lakeshore. Some patchy fog is possible for the central and \neastern U.P. after the passage of any thunderstorm, potentially \nlingering into the early morning hours Tuesday. The western U.P. can \nexpect clearing skies and falling humidities overnight. \n\nThe upper low will shift east on Tuesday into central Ontario. \nSignificant drying noted on Advanced Layer Precipitable Water \nimagery will spread into the area, with PWATs falling to around 0.75 \ninches. Deep mixing into this dry atmosphere is forecast, with \nrelative humidity values falling into the upper 20s/low 30s (%) over \nthe west half, with wind gusts of around 20 kt in the afternoon. In \nconsultation with fire weather partners, will hold off from issuing \nan SPS at this time due to unsupportive fuel indices. It will be \nwarm with highs ranging through the 80s, 10-15 degrees above normal \nin most spots. It will also continue to be fairly humid for the east \nhalf with wind flow off of Lake Michigan. \n\nWeak warm frontogenesis will occur Tuesday night/Wednesday morning \nto our south as PWATs increase again to 1.25-1.50 inches, with \nguidance mixed on whether associated rainfall will reach far enough \nnorth into the UP. Best chances (up to 60%) over the south. \n\nFor late week into the Labor Day weekend, ridging builds over the \nnorthern Plains, while a deep trough evolves over the Canadian \nMaritimes. While this pattern is generally favorable for subsidence \nover the local area, as evidenced by a high pressure ridge building \nin from the northeast, western parts of the UP may be close enough \nto the moisture/instability reservoir to see convection at times. \nCurrently capping out at 20-30% PoPs though, so the evolution is far \nfrom certain.",
